-- title:
--   Proper 𝒪-schemes: R-points agree with C-points
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, $K_0$ its fraction field, $\pi \in \mathcal O$, and let $C$ be a field equipped with a $K_0$-algebra structure and a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$. Let $R$ be a commutative ring that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a $C$-subalgebra source, with $C$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra, all compatibly (scalar towers $\mathcal O \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$). Let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformizer of $K_0$ in $C$, that is an element $\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N \in \mathbb N$. Assume `IsAdicFrame` $\pi\ \varpi\ R$: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly $\{c \in C : v(c) \le 1\}$, $R$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, an element $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(a) \le 1$ in $C$ precisely when it comes from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the images of $\pi$ and of $\varpi$ in $C$ coincide. Finally let $X$ be a scheme and $f : X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ a proper morphism. Then the map sending a morphism $p : \operatorname{Spec} R \to X$ with $f \circ p = \operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $\mathcal O \to R$ to $p \circ \operatorname{Spec}(R \to C)$ is injective, and every $cpt : \operatorname{Spec} C \to X$ with $f \circ cpt = \operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O \to C$ is of this form, i.e. equals $p \circ \operatorname{Spec}(R \to C)$ for some such $p$.
--
--   This is the valuative criterion of properness in the form used for the Čerednik–Drinfeld frame: for a proper $\mathcal O$-scheme, restriction along $\operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ identifies the $R$-points of $X$ over $\mathcal O$ with its $C$-points, the two halves of the bijection being stated separately as injectivity and surjectivity. It is used to produce integral points of the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient from points over the valued field $C$.
